 <LayoutItem background="false" blendMode="0" excludeFromExports="0" frame="false" frameJoinStyle="miter" groupUuid="" halign="1" htmlState="1" id="" itemRotation="0" labelText="&lt;div style=&quot;font-family: 'Segoe UI', Arial, sans-serif; background-color: #fdfdfd; border: 1px solid #d1d5db; border-left: 6px solid #4f46e5; padding: 12px 15px; border-radius: 6px; box-shadow: 2px 2px 5px rgba(0,0,0,0.05); color: #374151; line-height: 1.6;&quot;>&#xa;  &lt;h3 style=&quot;margin: 0 0 8px 0; color: #D946EF; font-size: 16px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.5px; border-bottom: 1px solid #e5e7eb; padding-bottom: 5px;&quot;>&#xa;    📝 Project Overview &amp; Significance&#xa;  &lt;/h3>&#xa;  &lt;p style=&quot;margin: 0; font-size: 14px; text-align: justify;&quot;>&#xa;    This geospatial study presents a comparative assessment of post-monsoon inundation and persistent waterlogging in &lt;b>Barpeta district, Assam&lt;/b> between 2024 and 2025. Utilizing high-resolution Sentinel-2 multispectral imagery captured uniformly on &lt;b>October 22&lt;/b>, surface water was delineated using the &lt;b>Normalized Difference Water Index (NDWI)&lt;/b> alongside advanced SCL cloud-masking techniques. The quantitative analysis demonstrates a &lt;b>20.1% reduction&lt;/b> in waterlogged area—decreasing from &lt;b>353.11 Sq. Km. (2024)&lt;/b> to &lt;b>281.98 Sq. Km. (2025)&lt;/b>. This study effectively captures prolonged water retention in low-lying topography and regional wetland ecosystems (&lt;i>Beels&lt;/i>), providing essential baseline data for flood drainage management and agricultural mitigation planning.&#xa;  &lt;/p>&#xa;&lt;/div>" marginX="0" marginY="0" opacity="1" outlineWidthM="0.3,mm" position="6.70654,7.99812,mm" positionLock="false" positionOnPage="6.70654,7.99812,mm" referencePoint="0" size="108.486,66.384,mm" templateUuid="{c4ae5354-32ae-40a4-93c0-bf0a1e260749}" type="65641" uuid="{c4ae5354-32ae-40a4-93c0-bf0a1e260749}" valign="32" visibility="1" zValue="11">
